  wake:"Shimokitazawa", sleep:"London (tonight, local time)", pace:"Gentle, pre-planned, unheroic", walking:"Minimal", anchor:"BA6 to London Heathrow, 13:05 from Haneda Terminal 3 (BOOKED)",
  summary:"The journey ends at Haneda, not with one last cross-city dash. Neighbourhood breakfast, checkout, the pre-booked taxi (or the taxi+Keikyu hybrid), Terminal 3 by 10:00 for the 13:05 BA6 nonstop to Heathrow — landing 19:50 the same evening, London time. No museum, no shopping district, no observation deck: protecting the memory of last night is today's whole job.",
  about:"Haneda was Tokyo's original 1931 airfield and is again its main international gateway — closer, calmer and better-designed than Narita for this route. One floor above the check-in hall (4F) is a recreated Edo-period street, Edo Koji — before security, so it happens between the bag drop and the gate — the last odd bit of architecture on the trip.", deeper:"<b>Haneda was Tokyo's original airport</b>, opened in 1931, and lost its international traffic to Narita in 1978 for political rather than practical reasons — Narita was built inland against violent local opposition that lasted decades. Haneda only regained major international routes in 2010, which is why its international terminal is new and unusually good.<br><br><b>Edo Koji, the 4F street, is a reconstruction of a Nihonbashi merchant street</b> — the point from which all distances in Japan were, and still are, measured. Every road sign giving a distance to Tokyo means the bridge at Nihonbashi. It is a reasonable last thing to stand in.",
  notice:["Reconfirm BA6 departs T3 (it does — international) and check in online 24h before — that's Sunday lunchtime, set a reminder.","Book the taxi TO SHINAGAWA for 08:45, 1–2 days ahead (GO app or host) — Shimokita's lanes are narrow; set the pickup pin precisely.","Keep passports + one battery pack in the day bag; the Kappabashi knife goes in CHECKED luggage."],
